              intellection are not accompanied by correlated physical events but
              there are physical events that parallel intellection and that
              intellection is independent of the body because intellection is an
              operation of the mind alone. This chapter explains Descartes
              mechanistic explanations as covering all animal behavior as a
              support to the immortality of the human soul with the human soul as
              different from the animal soul since human soul is immortal. This
              chapter also explains that the animal soul dies with the animal but
              it is thought to have some degree of reason, while the human soul
              is the principle of all the variety of functions ascribed to humans
              and the power of intellection. In intellection the soul operates
              independently of the body. The soul is the form of the body. It is
              also dependent on the body but at the same time it is an
              incorporeal body. Further the chapter explains Descartes
              mechanistic science which means that operations traditionally
              ascribed to the soul except the intellect can be explained
              mechanistically and does not need a soul.}}

